IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `crtpa-kleene`
IMPORT `crtpa-nouns`

-- Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 2001 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal
-- consolidation -- LAYER 1, SECTION 2: RIGHT OF THIRD PARTY TO ENFORCE CONTRACTUAL TERM.
--
-- What is encoded: s 2(1) to (7), the right itself (the two routes (a) and (b)), the exclusion in (2),
-- the identification in (3), the limit in (4), the remedies in (5), the exclusion-clause reading in (6)
-- and the two role definitions in (7).  Each subsection answers on its own (option C of ruling E1); the
-- gathering of the routes into one answer is by hand, in `crtpa-enforce`.
--
-- READING A RESULT.  JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act itself gives; NOTHING means the Act does
-- not answer on these facts (NOTES.md, "Reading a result: FALSE and NOTHING").  A fact the person may not be
-- able to state, or a judgment the text hands to the court or to the construction of the contract, is a
-- MAYBE BOOLEAN input, and NOTHING in it gives NOTHING out unless another limb settles the answer.

§ `Section 2(1) -- the person is a third party; the two routes`

-- src:61 | 2.—(1) Subject to the provisions of this Act, a person who is not a
-- src:62 | party to a contract (called in this Act a third party) may, in the third
-- src:63 | party’s own right, enforce a term of the contract if —
@ref s 2(1) "third party" (src:61-63)
GIVEN `the person is a party to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1) the person is a third party` MEANS
    IF `the person is a party to the contract`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person who is not a party to a contract (called in this Act a third party)"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- src:65 | (a) the contract expressly provides that the third party may; or
@ref s 2(1)(a) (src:65-65)
GIVEN `the contract expressly provides that the third party may enforce the term` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 2(1)(a) the contract expressly provides that the third party may enforce the term` MEANS
    `the contract expressly provides that the third party may enforce the term`

-- src:70 | (2) Subsection (1)(b) does not apply if, on a proper construction of
-- src:71 | the contract, it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be
-- src:72 | enforceable by the third party.
@ref s 2(2) (src:70-72)
GIVEN `on a proper construction of the contract it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(2) subsection (1)(b) does not apply to the term` MEANS
    CONSIDER `on a proper construction of the contract it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (a failed "if", read as the text's no within the provision that holds it; marginal reading M2): "does not apply if, on a proper construction of the contract, it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party"
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "on a proper construction of the contract, it appears" is a judgment of construction the text leaves open, and the person has not stated it
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:67 | (b) subject to subsection (2), the term purports to confer a
-- src:68 | benefit on the third party.
@ref s 2(1)(b) (src:67-68)
GIVEN `the term purports to confer a benefit on the third party` IS A BOOLEAN
      `on a proper construction of the contract it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(1)(b) the term purports to confer a benefit on the third party and subsection (2) does not take it out` MEANS
    `three-valued AND`
        (`a stated fact` `the term purports to confer a benefit on the third party`)
        (`three-valued NOT` (`s 2(2) subsection (1)(b) does not apply to the term` `on a proper construction of the contract it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party`))

-- src:61 | 2.—(1) Subject to the provisions of this Act, a person who is not a
-- src:62 | party to a contract (called in this Act a third party) may, in the third
-- src:63 | party’s own right, enforce a term of the contract if —
-- src:65 | (a) the contract expressly provides that the third party may; or
-- src:67 | (b) subject to subsection (2), the term purports to confer a
-- src:68 | benefit on the third party.
@ref s 2(1) (src:61-68)
GIVEN `the person is a party to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the contract expressly provides that the third party may enforce the term` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the term purports to confer a benefit on the third party` IS A BOOLEAN
      `on a proper construction of the contract it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
-- "Subject to the provisions of this Act": the other provisions are asked in their own rules and gathered in `crtpa-enforce`.
-- FALSE-kind (a failed "if", read as the text's no; marginal reading M1): "may, in the third party's own right, enforce a term of the contract if — (a) the contract expressly provides that the third party may; or (b) subject to subsection (2), the term purports to confer a benefit on the third party."
-- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): when route (a) is not made out and the judgment of s 2(2) has not been stated, the answer is NOTHING
`s 2(1) the third party may in the third party's own right enforce the term of the contract` MEANS
    `three-valued AND`
        (`s 2(1) the person is a third party` `the person is a party to the contract`)
        (`three-valued OR`
            (`a stated fact` (`s 2(1)(a) the contract expressly provides that the third party may enforce the term` `the contract expressly provides that the third party may enforce the term`))
            (`s 2(1)(b) the term purports to confer a benefit on the third party and subsection (2) does not take it out` `the term purports to confer a benefit on the third party` `on a proper construction of the contract it appears that the parties did not intend the term to be enforceable by the third party`))

§ `Section 2(3) -- identification of the third party`

-- Judgment call QB (NOTES.md): "must be expressly identified" is read as a condition of the right, set
-- against "but need not be in existence", and so not being identified is the text's no (JUST FALSE).
-- The default reading (a bare "must" answers NOTHING) is the other reading.
-- src:74 | (3) The third party must be expressly identified in the contract by
-- src:75 | name, as a member of a class or as answering a particular description,
-- src:76 | but need not be in existence when the contract is entered into.
@ref s 2(3) (src:74-75)
GIVEN `how the third party is identified in the contract` IS A `Way the third party is identified in the contract`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) the third party is expressly identified in the contract by name, as a member of a class or as answering a particular description` MEANS
    CONSIDER `how the third party is identified in the contract`
    WHEN `expressly, by name` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `expressly, as a member of a class` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `expressly, as answering a particular description` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (a condition of the right, judgment call QB): "The third party must be expressly identified in the contract by name, as a member of a class or as answering a particular description"
    WHEN `by implication only` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- FALSE-kind (a condition of the right, judgment call QB): "The third party must be expressly identified in the contract by name, as a member of a class or as answering a particular description"
    WHEN `not identified at all` THEN JUST FALSE

@ref s 2(3) (src:76-76)
GIVEN `the third party was in existence when the contract was entered into` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(3) the third party must be in existence when the contract is entered into` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "but need not be in existence when the contract is entered into"
    JUST FALSE

§ `Section 2(4) -- subject to the other relevant terms of the contract`

-- src:78 | (4) This section does not confer a right on a third party to enforce a
-- src:79 | term of a contract otherwise than subject to and in accordance with
-- src:80 | any other relevant terms of the contract.
@ref s 2(4) (src:78-80)
GIVEN `the third party's enforcement would be subject to and in accordance with the other relevant terms of the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(4) section 2 confers a right on the third party to enforce the term otherwise than subject to and in accordance with the other relevant terms of the contract` MEANS
    IF `the third party's enforcement would be subject to and in accordance with the other relevant terms of the contract`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the negative reaches only enforcement "otherwise than subject to and in accordance with" the other terms; enforcement in accordance with them is outside it
    THEN NOTHING
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This section does not confer a right on a third party to enforce a term of a contract otherwise than subject to and in accordance with any other relevant terms of the contract."
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Section 2(5) -- remedies`

-- src:82 | (5) For the purpose of exercising a third party’s right to enforce a
-- src:83 | term of the contract, there is to be available to the third party any
-- src:84 | remedy that would have been available to the third party in an action
-- src:85 | for breach of contract if the third party had been a party to the contract
-- src:86 | (and the rules relating to damages, injunctions, specific performance
-- src:87 | and other remedy apply accordingly) and such remedy must not be
-- src:88 | refused on the ground that, as against the promisor, the third party is a
-- src:89 | volunteer.
@ref s 2(5) (src:82-86)
GIVEN `the third party has a right to enforce the term` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the remedy would have been available to the third party in an action for breach of contract if the third party had been a party to the contract` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(5) the remedy is available to the third party` MEANS
    IF `the third party has a right to enforce the term` EQUALS JUST TRUE
       AND `the remedy would have been available to the third party in an action for breach of contract if the third party had been a party to the contract`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the grant is "for the purpose of exercising a third party's right", of "any remedy that would have been available" to a party; outside those limits the text does not answer
    ELSE NOTHING

@ref s 2(5) the rules of remedy (src:86-87)
GIVEN `the third party has a right to enforce the term` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(5) the rules relating to damages, injunctions, specific performance and other remedy apply to the third party's remedy accordingly` MEANS
    IF `the third party has a right to enforce the term` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    -- TRUE: the text's own "(and the rules relating to damages, injunctions, specific performance and other remedy apply accordingly)"; the rules themselves are not among the sources
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the parenthesis is part of the grant "for the purpose of exercising a third party's right to enforce a term"; where the third party has no such right the text does not say the rules apply
    ELSE NOTHING

@ref s 2(5) (src:87-89)
GIVEN `the ground for refusing the remedy is that, as against the promisor, the third party is a volunteer` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(5) the remedy may be refused on the ground that, as against the promisor, the third party is a volunteer` MEANS
    IF `the ground for refusing the remedy is that, as against the promisor, the third party is a volunteer`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "such remedy must not be refused on the ground that, as against the promisor, the third party is a volunteer"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the prohibition reaches only refusal "on the ground that, as against the promisor, the third party is a volunteer"; refusal on another ground is outside it
    ELSE NOTHING

§ `Section 2(6) -- terms that exclude or limit liability`

-- src:91 | (6) Where a term of a contract excludes or limits liability in relation
-- src:92 | to any matter, references in this Act to the third party enforcing the
-- src:93 | term are to be construed as references to the third party availing
-- src:94 | himself, herself or itself of the exclusion or limitation.
@ref s 2(6) (src:91-94)
GIVEN `the kind of term` IS A `Kind of term`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(6) references in this Act to the third party enforcing the term are to be construed as references to availing of the exclusion or limitation` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of term`
    WHEN `a term that excludes or limits liability in relation to a matter` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (a failed "where"; the periods-and-where ruling of 2026-11-05, rule (b)): "Where a term of a contract excludes or limits liability in relation to any matter, references in this Act to the third party enforcing the term are to be construed as references to the third party availing himself, herself or itself of the exclusion or limitation."
    WHEN `any other term` THEN JUST FALSE

@ref s 2(6) (src:91-94)
GIVEN `the kind of term` IS A `Kind of term`
GIVETH A MAYBE `Meaning of a reference to enforcing a term`
`s 2(6) what a reference to the third party enforcing the term is construed as` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind of term`
    WHEN `a term that excludes or limits liability in relation to a matter` THEN JUST `availing of the exclusion or limitation`
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the text gives no other construction for a term that does not exclude or limit liability; the yes/no function above carries the "where" that fails
    WHEN `any other term` THEN NOTHING

§ `Section 2(7) -- promisee and promisor`

-- src:96 | (7) In this Act, in relation to a term of a contract which is
-- src:97 | enforceable by a third party —
-- src:99 | “promisee” means the party to the contract by whom the term is
-- src:100 | enforceable against the promisor;
@ref s 2(7) "promisee" (src:96-100)
GIVEN `the term is enforceable by a third party` IS A BOOLEAN
      `where the party stands` IS A `Position of a party in relation to the term`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(7) the party is the promisee` MEANS
    IF `the term is enforceable by a third party`
    THEN CONSIDER `where the party stands`
         WHEN `the party by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor` THEN JUST TRUE
         -- FALSE-kind (a definition that says "means"): "“promisee” means the party to the contract by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor"
         WHEN `the party against whom the term is enforceable by the third party` THEN JUST FALSE
         -- FALSE-kind (a definition that says "means"): "“promisee” means the party to the contract by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor"
         WHEN `a party in neither of those positions` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the definitions are "in relation to a term of a contract which is enforceable by a third party" and say nothing of any other term
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:96 | (7) In this Act, in relation to a term of a contract which is
-- src:97 | enforceable by a third party —
-- src:102 | “promisor” means the party to the contract against whom the
-- src:103 | term is enforceable by the third party.
@ref s 2(7) "promisor" (src:96-97) (src:102-103)
GIVEN `the term is enforceable by a third party` IS A BOOLEAN
      `where the party stands` IS A `Position of a party in relation to the term`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 2(7) the party is the promisor` MEANS
    IF `the term is enforceable by a third party`
    THEN CONSIDER `where the party stands`
         WHEN `the party against whom the term is enforceable by the third party` THEN JUST TRUE
         -- FALSE-kind (a definition that says "means"): "“promisor” means the party to the contract against whom the term is enforceable by the third party"
         WHEN `the party by whom the term is enforceable against the promisor` THEN JUST FALSE
         -- FALSE-kind (a definition that says "means"): "“promisor” means the party to the contract against whom the term is enforceable by the third party"
         WHEN `a party in neither of those positions` THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the definitions are "in relation to a term of a contract which is enforceable by a third party" and say nothing of any other term
    ELSE NOTHING
